Remove Linked List Elements (LeetCode 203)

Optimal Solution

Approach:
Traverse the linked list using a pointer temp.
Keep another pointer prev to store the previous node.
If the current node value is equal to val:
Store the next node.
If the node is the head node, update head.
Otherwise, connect prev->next to the next node.
Delete the current node.
Move temp to the next node.
If the current node value is not equal to val:
Move both prev and temp forward.

Idea: Whenever the target value is found, remove that node and reconnect the remaining list.

Time Complexity: O(n)
We traverse the linked list only once.

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra data structure is used.

Code : 

/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * struct ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode *next;
 *     ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
 *     ListNode(int x, ListNode *next) : val(x), next(next) {}
 * };
 */

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* removeElements(ListNode* head, int val) {

        ListNode* temp = head;
        ListNode* prev = nullptr;

        while (temp != nullptr) {

            // If current node contains target value
            if (temp->val == val) {

                ListNode* nextNode = temp->next;

                // If target node is the head
                if (prev == nullptr) {
                    head = nextNode;
                }
                else {
                    prev->next = nextNode;
                }

                delete temp;

                // Move to next node
                temp = nextNode;
            }

            else {
                // Move both pointers
                prev = temp;
                temp = temp->next;
            }
        }

        return head;
    }
};
